## 1. IMPLEMENTATION — lazy vm_space growth (3 vendored files + 1 run fix)

### 1.1 Design rationale (why LAZY, why LEAF=9)

Prior eager-growth attempts (p4-cspace-fix.log §4) ALL broke boot:
  A. NUM_CNODE_3RD 3→6 eager: _cnodes[64] array (~84 KiB in Platform_pd)
     starved core's heap → "PD session unavailable".
  B. Lazy + NUM_CNODE_3RD=6: the _cnodes[64] ARRAY is part of the
     Platform_pd object regardless of lazy construction → still starved.
  C. LEAF=9 + NUM_CNODE_3RD=4 lazy: silent Platform() crash (the raw
     virt_size = 2*max_page_frames()*PAGE_SIZE = 2 GiB, eagerly backed
     with page tables during Platform()).

LESSON (from the task + prior art): growth must be LAZY (construct on
first use), the _cnodes[] array must stay SMALL, and core's virt
reservation must be CAPPED.

The chosen config resolves all three:
  - LEAF=9 + NUM_CNODE_3RD=3 (unchanged) → NUM_VM_SEL_LOG2=17 (131072),
    _cnodes[8] array UNCHANGED (no Platform_pd object growth beyond the
    Selector_allocator's 4 KiB → 16 KiB bump). Platform_pd ≈ 35 KiB,
    well under attempt A's 84 KiB failure point.
  - _ensure_leaf() constructs 3rd-level and leaf CNodes on first map
    into their selector range. At PD creation, only _vm_pad_cnode (the
    2nd-level root) is constructed. Zero eager per-PD cost for leaves.
  - platform.cc caps core's virt reservation to 256 MiB on x86_64,
    avoiding the 1 GiB page-table reservation that killed Platform().

### 1.2 vm_space.h — lazy construction + enlarged leaves

  LEAF_CNODE_SIZE_LOG2: 7 → 9 on x86_64 (#ifdef __x86_64__, other arches
    keep upstream byte-for-byte). Each leaf now has 512 entries (16 KiB
    backing, drawn from the 16K pool via the C1 cnode_backing_alloc path).

  + Cap_sel const _core_cnode_sel: retained from the constructor so that
    _ensure_leaf() can place lazily-constructed CNodes in core's CSpace
    long after the constructor returns.

  + _ensure_leaf(l3, l4): the lazy construction hook. On first access to
    a selector in group (l3, l4):
      1. If _cnodes[l3]._3rd is not constructed: construct it (4 KiB-
         backed, VM_3RD_CNODE_SIZE_LOG2=7) and copy its cap into
         _vm_pad_cnode at index l3.
      2. If _cnodes[l3]._4th[l4] is not constructed: construct it (16 KiB-
         backed, LEAF_CNODE_SIZE_LOG2=9) and copy its cap into the 3rd-
         level CNode at index l4.
    Called under _mutex (all call sites: map/map_guest/unmap/alloc_page_
    tables hold _mutex; destructor is single-threaded). Thread-safe.

  _leaf_cnode(): now calls _ensure_leaf(l3, l4) before with_cnode(fn).
    If construction fails (16K pool exhausted, sel alloc denied), the
    leaf stays unconstructed, with_cnode returns false, and _map_frame
    returns DENIED — fail-loud (error("Cnode construction failed") is
    logged by the Cnode ctor), bounded (map fails, no silent hang).

  Construct_cnode::destruct: made no-op-safe (early return if
    !constructed()). The destructor iterates the whole _cnodes[] array
    uniformly; unconstructed (never-used) entries are skipped.

  constructed(): simplified to return _vm_pad_cnode.constructed() (with
    lazy construction, only the 2nd-level pad needs to be constructed
    for the PD to be viable; leaves are on-demand).

  Constructor: only _vm_pad_cnode is constructed + inserted into
    top_level_cnode. The eager _for_each_l3_cnodes loop (which built
    all 8*32=256 leaves eagerly) is REMOVED.

  Destructor: the _revert_cnodes_l3 block is restructured so
    vm_pad_cnode.remove(l3) and the leaf/3rd destructs happen INSIDE
    cnode_3rd.with_cnode() (only for constructed groups). Unconstructed
    groups are skipped via the no-op-safe destruct.

  _for_each_l3_cnodes: removed (unused after the eager loop and the
    constructed() simplification).

### 1.3 platform.cc — cap core's virt reservation

  The raw computation 2 * max_page_frames() * PAGE_SIZE at
  NUM_VM_SEL_LOG2=17 yields 1 GiB. Platform() eagerly reserves this
  and backs it with page tables — this is what silently killed
  Platform() in prior attempt C. Capped to 256 MiB (the upstream x86_64
  value at NUM_VM_SEL_LOG2=15) via #ifdef __x86_64__. Core's own dynamic
  usage needs only 256 MiB; other arches keep the upstream formula.

### 1.4 spec/x86_64/platform.cc — 16K pool budget for vm leaves

  The 16K pool now covers a third consumer: lazily-constructed vm leaves
  (16 KiB each, LEAF_CNODE_SIZE_LOG2=9). Added MAX_VM_LEAF_COUNT=2048
  (2048 * 16 KiB = 32 MiB). Total pool budget:
    16 VCPUs * 16 KiB  (upstream)
    + 64 PDs * 64 main-CSpace 2nd-level CNodes * 16 KiB  (C1)
    + 2048 vm leaves * 16 KiB  (C2, NEW)
    = ~96 MiB (was ~64 MiB in C1, well under the 247 MiB that broke boot)
  The vm leaf term is consumed lazily (only as PDs actually map into
  new selector ranges), NOT at boot.

### 1.5 run/sponge-falkon-disk.run — rom_pkg caps config fix (Sponge-owned)

  rom_pkg (cached_fs_rom serving falkon's 509 MiB WebEngine payload from
  disk) had caps: 2000 — enough for its page_table_registry slab to
  track only ~3000 page-frame entries before exhausting its PD cap quota
  ("out of CAP"). Bumped to caps: 20000. This is NOT a vendored change
  (run/ is Sponge-owned); it was always undersized but the C1 vm_space
  "out of selector" hit FIRST, masking it. With C2 fixing vm_space,
  rom_pkg's cap limit became the next exposed limit; this bump lets
  rom_pkg serve the full payload cleanly (zero "out of CAP" in run 2).

